[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114845,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cherubism is a rare autosomal dominant&ndash;inherited bone disorder characterized by symmetrical expansion of the mandible, maxilla, or both, by an osteoclast-like giant cell&ndash;rich lesion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114846,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114847,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LD24.22 Cherubism</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114848,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> familial fibrous dysplasia; juvenile fibrous dysplasia.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114849,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114850,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Both jaws are affected bilaterally, with the mandible more commonly affected than the maxilla {23298620}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114851,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "<p>Slow, symmetrical expansion of the jaws develops in early childhood, usually before 6&nbsp;years of age. Maxillary expansion in severe cases may cause orbital displacement with scleral exposure, producing the characteristic facial appearance similar to that of putti or cherubs in Renaissance paintings. Female patients are usually more severely affected. Tooth displacement and impaction, root resorption, and agenesis are typical. Partial or complete regression occurs by adulthood {25409853}.</p>\r\n<p>An association with odontogenic tumours is reported {28721660}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>Radiologically, affected bones show a symmetrical, well-defined, radiolucent/hypodense, multilocular, soap bubble&ndash;like appearance, with thinned cortex {32620450}. The fibrous tissue is progressively replaced by new bone, leading to sclerosis.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T17:36:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114852,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The incidence is unknown. Two&nbsp;thirds of cases are familial, with complete penetrance in males and 60&ndash;70% penetrance in females, and with variable expressivity; the remaining cases arise as de&nbsp;novo germline alterations {32620450; 28644570}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114853,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "<p>Autosomal dominant <em>SH3BP2</em> mutations, located on chromosome 4p16.3 {10364527; 10364528}, have been identified in about 90% of cases {32620450}. The majority of mutations occur in exon&nbsp;9, within a proline-rich sequence six amino acids long {11381256}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T17:36:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114854,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The <em>SH3BP2</em> mutations result in the excessive generation of abnormally large osteoclasts with constitutive activity {30236129}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114855,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is similar to that of other giant cell lesions, with red and haemorrhagic tissue and areas of cystic change.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114856,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The microscopic features are not specific and resemble those of other giant cell&ndash;rich lesions of the jaws, including giant cell granuloma, giant cell lesions in Noonan and Jaffe&ndash;Campanacci syndromes {31705763}, and brown tumour of hyperparathyroidism. Typically, abnormally large multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells lie in a monotonous loose or oedematous spindle cell stroma with eosinophilic cuff-like perivascular deposits {25409853}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114857,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "<p>Not clinically relevant {20715662}</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T17:37:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114858,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114859,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> only the posterior jaws affected; bilateral symmetrical lesions; giant cell lesion on histology.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> detection of <em>SH3BP2</em> germline mutation (in selected cases).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T17:37:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114860,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114861,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cases regress after puberty, but some show continued growth with little regression. Surgery should be performed only in severe cases, where it will provide a functional benefit {23298620}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]